@@@ problem 20 valid-parentheses
title: Valid Parentheses
difficulty: Easy
topics: String, Stack
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=s.length

=== description
Given a string `s` containing only the characters `'('`, `')'`, `'{'`, `'}'`, `'['` and `']'`, decide whether it is **valid**:

1. every opening bracket is closed by a bracket of the same type;
2. brackets are closed in the correct order;
3. every closing bracket has a matching opening bracket.

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length <= 10^4`
- `s` consists of parentheses only: `()[]{}`

=== hints
- The most recently opened bracket must be closed first. Which data structure gives you "most recent first"?

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string s) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::isValid);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        const std::string open = "([{", close = ")]}";
        std::string s, st;
        int half = std::max(1, n / 2);
        while ((int)s.size() < 2 * half) {
            int remaining = 2 * half - (int)s.size();
            if (!st.empty() && ((int)st.size() >= remaining || g.coin(0.5))) {
                s += close[open.find(st.back())];
                st.pop_back();
            } else {
                char c = open[g.irange(0, 2)];
                s += c;
                st += c;
            }
        }
        if (g.coin(0.4)) s[g.irange(0, (int)s.size() - 1)] = "()[]{}"[g.irange(0, 5)];
        if (g.coin(0.1)) s.pop_back();
        return lc::lines(s);
    });
}

=== approach
Use a **stack** of open brackets. Push every opening bracket. For a closing bracket, the stack top must be its matching opener; pop it, otherwise the string is invalid. At the end the stack must be empty (no unclosed brackets).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string s) {
        string stack;                                   // pending opening brackets
        for (char c : s) {
            if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {
                stack.push_back(c);
            } else {
                char want = c == ')' ? '(' : c == ']' ? '[' : '{';
                if (stack.empty() || stack.back() != want) return false;
                stack.pop_back();                       // matched the latest opener
            }
        }
        return stack.empty();                           // nothing left unclosed
    }
};

=== examples
"()"
> true

"()[]{}"
> true

"(]"
> false

"([])"
> true

=== tests
"["
> ?

"]"
> ?

"([)]"
> ?

"{[]}"
> ?

"(("
> ?

@@@ problem 71 simplify-path
title: Simplify Path
difficulty: Medium
topics: String, Stack
params: path
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=path.length

=== description
You are given an **absolute** Unix-style path (it starts with `/`). Convert it to its **canonical** form:

- `.` means the current directory and `..` the parent directory (the parent of `/` is `/` itself);
- several consecutive slashes count as a single slash;
- any other name, including names made of dots such as `...`, is a normal directory name.

The canonical path starts with a single `/`, separates directories with a single `/`, has no trailing `/` (unless it is the root), and contains no `.` or `..` components.

=== constraints
- `1 <= path.length <= 3000`
- `path` contains English letters, digits, `.`, `/` and `_`
- `path` is a valid absolute Unix path

=== hints
- Split the path on `/` and ignore empty parts.
- Process parts with a stack: a name is pushed, `..` pops (if possible), `.` does nothing.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    string simplifyPath(string path) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::simplifyPath);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::string> parts = {"a", "bc", "..", ".", "...", "x_y", "home", "..", "d1"};
        std::string p;
        while ((int)p.size() < std::max(1, n)) p += std::string(g.irange(1, 2), '/') + g.pick(parts);
        if (g.coin(0.3)) p += "/";
        return lc::lines(p);
    });
}

=== approach
Split the path on `/` and process each non-empty component with a **stack** of directory names:

- `.` is ignored;
- `..` pops the top directory if there is one;
- anything else is pushed.

Finally join the stack as `"/" + d1 + "/" + d2 ...`, or `"/"` if the stack is empty.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    string simplifyPath(string path) {
        vector<string> dirs;                    // stack of directory names
        int n = path.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < n;) {
            while (i < n && path[i] == '/') ++i;             // skip slashes
            int j = i;
            while (j < n && path[j] != '/') ++j;             // [i, j) is a component
            string part = path.substr(i, j - i);
            i = j;
            if (part.empty() || part == ".") continue;
            if (part == "..") {
                if (!dirs.empty()) dirs.pop_back();          // go to parent
            } else {
                dirs.push_back(part);                        // enter directory
            }
        }
        if (dirs.empty()) return "/";
        string out;
        for (auto &d : dirs) out += "/" + d;
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
"/home/"
> "/home"

"/home//foo/"
> "/home/foo"

"/home/user/Documents/../Pictures"
> "/home/user/Pictures"

"/../"
> "/"
: The parent of the root is the root.

"/.../a/../b/c/../d/./"
> "/.../b/d"
: "..." is an ordinary directory name.

=== tests
"/"
> ?

"/a/./b/../../c/"
> ?

"/a//b////c/d//././/.."
> ?

"/..hidden/.."
> ?

@@@ problem 155 min-stack
title: Min Stack
difficulty: Medium
topics: Stack, Design
params: operations, arguments
time: O(1) per operation
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=number_of_operations

=== description
Design a stack that supports push, pop, top, and retrieving the **minimum** element, all in **O(1)** time.

- `MinStack()` initializes the stack.
- `void push(int val)` pushes `val`.
- `void pop()` removes the top element.
- `int top()` returns the top element.
- `int getMin()` returns the minimum element in the stack.

`pop`, `top` and `getMin` are only called on non-empty stacks.

=== constraints
- `-2^31 <= val <= 2^31 - 1`
- at most `3 * 10^4` calls in total

=== hints
- Store, next to each element, the minimum of the stack *at the time it was pushed*.

=== template
class MinStack {
public:
    MinStack() {

    }

    void push(int val) {

    }

    void pop() {

    }

    int top() {

    }

    int getMin() {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.design<MinStack>()
        .method("push", &MinStack::push)
        .method("pop", &MinStack::pop)
        .method("top", &MinStack::top)
        .method("getMin", &MinStack::getMin);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        lc::Ops ops;
        ops.add("MinStack");
        int size = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            int r = g.irange(0, 9);
            if (size == 0 || r < 4) { ops.add("push", g.irange(-1000, 1000)); ++size; }
            else if (r < 6) { ops.add("pop"); --size; }
            else if (r < 8) ops.add("getMin");
            else ops.add("top");
        }
        return ops.lines();
    });
}

=== approach
Store pairs `(value, minSoFar)` on a single stack, where `minSoFar` is the minimum of this element and everything below it. Pushing computes `min(val, previous minSoFar)`; popping removes the pair, automatically restoring the previous minimum. `top` and `getMin` just read the top pair.

=== solution
class MinStack {
    // Each entry remembers the minimum of the stack from the bottom up to itself.
    vector<pair<int, int>> st;   // (value, minimum so far)

public:
    MinStack() {}

    void push(int val) {
        int m = st.empty() ? val : min(val, st.back().second);
        st.push_back({val, m});
    }

    void pop() { st.pop_back(); }

    int top() { return st.back().first; }

    int getMin() { return st.back().second; }
};

=== examples
["MinStack","push","push","push","getMin","pop","top","getMin"]
[[],[-2],[0],[-3],[],[],[],[]]
> [null,null,null,null,-3,null,0,-2]

=== tests
["MinStack","push","getMin","top"]
[[],[5],[],[]]
> ?

["MinStack","push","push","push","getMin","pop","getMin","pop","getMin"]
[[],[1],[1],[2],[],[],[],[],[]]
> ?

["MinStack","push","push","getMin","pop","push","getMin"]
[[],[2147483647],[-2147483648],[],[],[7],[]]
> ?

@@@ problem 150 evaluate-reverse-polish-notation
title: Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Math, Stack
params: tokens
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=number_of_tokens

=== description
Evaluate an arithmetic expression given in **Reverse Polish Notation** (postfix), as an array of string `tokens`.

- The operators are `+`, `-`, `*` and `/`; operands are integers.
- Division between two integers **truncates toward zero**.
- There is no division by zero, the expression is always valid, and every intermediate result fits in a 32-bit integer.

=== constraints
- `1 <= tokens.length <= 10^4`
- each token is an operator or an integer in `[-200, 200]`

=== hints
- Push numbers on a stack. An operator pops two operands (careful with the order!) and pushes the result.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int evalRPN(vector<string>& tokens) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::evalRPN);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::string> tok;
        std::vector<long long> st;
        int numbers = std::max(1, (n + 1) / 2);
        int pushed = 0;
        while (pushed < numbers || st.size() > 1) {
            bool canOp = st.size() >= 2;
            if (pushed < numbers && (!canOp || g.coin(0.55))) {
                long long v = g.irange(-200, 200);
                tok.push_back(std::to_string(v));
                st.push_back(v);
                ++pushed;
                continue;
            }
            long long b = st.back(); st.pop_back();
            long long a = st.back(); st.pop_back();
            std::vector<std::pair<char, long long>> ok;
            if (std::llabs(a + b) <= 100000000) ok.push_back({'+', a + b});
            if (std::llabs(a - b) <= 100000000) ok.push_back({'-', a - b});
            if (std::llabs(a * b) <= 100000000) ok.push_back({'*', a * b});
            if (b != 0) ok.push_back({'/', a / b});
            if (ok.empty()) ok.push_back({'*', 0}), b = 0;
            auto pick = g.pick(ok);
            tok.push_back(std::string(1, pick.first));
            st.push_back(pick.second);
        }
        return lc::lines(tok);
    });
}

=== approach
Scan the tokens with a **stack** of integers. Numbers are pushed. For an operator, pop `b` (the right operand, pushed last) and then `a` (the left operand), compute `a op b` and push the result. At the end, the single remaining value is the answer. C++ integer division already truncates toward zero.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int evalRPN(vector<string>& tokens) {
        vector<long long> st;
        for (auto &t : tokens) {
            if (t.size() == 1 && string("+-*/").find(t[0]) != string::npos) {
                long long b = st.back(); st.pop_back();   // right operand
                long long a = st.back(); st.pop_back();   // left operand
                switch (t[0]) {
                    case '+': st.push_back(a + b); break;
                    case '-': st.push_back(a - b); break;
                    case '*': st.push_back(a * b); break;
                    default:  st.push_back(a / b); break; // truncates toward zero
                }
            } else {
                st.push_back(stoll(t));                   // operand (may be negative)
            }
        }
        return (int)st.back();
    }
};

=== examples
["2","1","+","3","*"]
> 9
: ((2 + 1) * 3) = 9

["4","13","5","/","+"]
> 6
: (4 + (13 / 5)) = 6

["10","6","9","3","+","-11","*","/","*","17","+","5","+"]
> 22

=== tests
["18"]
> ?

["-7","2","/"]
> ?

["3","-4","-"]
> ?

["4","-2","/","2","-3","-","-"]
> ?

@@@ problem 224 basic-calculator
title: Basic Calculator
difficulty: Hard
topics: Math, String, Stack, Recursion
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=s.length

=== description
Given a string `s` representing a valid expression, evaluate it and return the result. You may **not** use built-in evaluation functions like `eval()`.

The expression contains non-negative integers, `+`, `-`, parentheses `(` `)` and spaces.

- `-` may be **unary** (e.g. `"-1"` or `"-(2 + 3)"`); `+` is never unary.
- Every number and every intermediate result fits in a signed 32-bit integer.

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length <= 3 * 10^5`
- `s` consists of digits, `'+'`, `'-'`, `'('`, `')'` and `' '`
- `s` is a valid expression

=== hints
- Without parentheses, keep a running `result` and the `sign` of the next number.
- On `(`, save the current `result` and `sign` on a stack and start fresh; on `)`, combine the inner result with the saved state.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int calculate(string s) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::calculate);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        // random expression with nested parentheses and unary minus
        std::string s;
        int depth = 0;
        bool expectOperand = true;
        while ((int)s.size() < std::max(3, n) || depth > 0 || expectOperand) {
            bool finishing = (int)s.size() >= std::max(3, n);
            if (expectOperand) {
                if (g.coin(0.15) && (s.empty() || s.back() == '(')) s += "-";   // unary minus
                if (!finishing && depth < 30 && g.coin(0.25)) { s += "("; ++depth; continue; }
                s += std::to_string(g.irange(0, 1000));
                expectOperand = false;
            } else if (depth > 0 && (finishing || g.coin(0.2))) {
                s += ")";
                --depth;
            } else {
                s += g.coin(0.5) ? " + " : (g.coin(0.5) ? "-" : " - ");
                expectOperand = true;
            }
        }
        return lc::lines(s);
    });
}

=== approach
Process the string left to right keeping:

- `result`: the value of the current parenthesis level so far;
- `sign`: `+1` or `-1` for the next number;
- a **stack** of saved `(result, sign)` pairs for enclosing levels.

Digits build a number; when it ends, add `sign * number` to `result`. `+`/`-` set the sign. On `(`, push the current `result` and `sign`, then reset both. On `)`, the inner value is `result`; pop the saved pair and set `result = savedResult + savedSign * result`. Unary minus falls out naturally: `-(…)` is just `0 - (…)`.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int calculate(string s) {
        long long result = 0, number = 0;
        int sign = 1;
        vector<pair<long long, int>> st;    // saved (result, sign) for outer levels
        for (char c : s) {
            if (isdigit(c)) {
                number = number * 10 + (c - '0');
            } else if (c == '+' || c == '-') {
                result += sign * number;    // finish the previous number
                number = 0;
                sign = c == '+' ? 1 : -1;
            } else if (c == '(') {
                st.push_back({result, sign});   // remember the outer level
                result = 0;
                sign = 1;
            } else if (c == ')') {
                result += sign * number;    // finish the inner expression
                number = 0;
                auto [outer, outerSign] = st.back();
                st.pop_back();
                result = outer + outerSign * result;
                sign = 1;
            }
            // spaces are ignored
        }
        return (int)(result + sign * number);
    }
};

=== examples
"1 + 1"
> 2

" 2-1 + 2 "
> 3

"(1+(4+5+2)-3)+(6+8)"
> 23

=== tests
"-1"
> ?

"-(2 + 3)"
> ?

"1-(     -2)"
> ?

"2147483647"
> ?

"(((7)))-((1-2)-(3-4))"
> ?
